 ## Background
-This method was developed as part of the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) architecture, an object-order volume rendering engine developed at the University of Mannheim and commercially distributed by Volume Graphics GmbH [S1, S2]. It was designed to address the high computational cost of geometry operations in software-based volume rendering, which accounted for 80% of computation time per projection [S1]. The architecture utilizes a farm of programmable Digital Signal Processors (DSPs) for the ray-casting portion, while the geometry unit handles the intensive resampling and gradient calculations in hardware [S1, S2].
